# Wolseley Hornet six-cylinder two-seater, 1930-1936.
#
# The 1,271 cc six-cylinder Hornet: the narrow grille, cycle wings and long
# bonnet. The pack's only true side view is an open two-seater, not the
# saloon, so the two-seater is modelled and the saloon is not represented.
# https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wolseley_Hornet
#
# Dimensions 3.581 x 1.397 x 1.600 m, wheelbase 2.413 m (saloon set).
#
# Shape reference: image 1 is a level side view of a 1932 six-cylinder car
# with a two-seater body; the other images show the front and detail. The
# notes record that the Commons title wrongly says 'Saloon'.
#
# Colours: grade (d); no period chart was found. Black, green, red and
# silver are plain names. Weights 5, 4, 3, 3, total 15, provisional.
#
# Speed 120 km/h. Comfort 45/398, on the line 330 + 1.5 x 45: an open
# two-seater. Classes 2,3.
# Dates are road presence: 1930 intro, retires staggered 1950-1953.
#
# Metres master: citycars/4.5/wolseley-hornet-1930.blend. Interchange:
# citycars/4.5/wolseley-hornet-1930.dae. References:
# citycars/references/wolseley-hornet-1930/ (gitignored; Wikimedia Commons).
#
# Accepted deviations: the model lacks the photographed wire spokes, and the
# cockpit cut-down and rear deck need closer shaping. The open two-seater is the
# body the side photograph shows; the saloon, Special and coupe are not
# represented. At 128 px the long bonnet, cycle wings and open cockpit read as a
# 1930s Hornet.


obj=citycar
name=WolseleyHornet1930(Black)
copyright=JamesPetts

DistributionWeight=5
intro_year=1930
intro_month=1
retire_year=1953
retire_month=6
speed=120
length=2
class=2,3
maximum_comfortable_journey_minutes=45
maximum_tolerable_journey_minutes=398

